.TH saveimage 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
saveimage, loadimage \- save and restore the screen as a BMP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B saveimage
.I file
[\fIx y w h\fR]
.br
.B loadimage
.I file
[\fIx\fR [\fIy\fR [\fImode\fR [\fIximage\fR [\fIyimage\fR]]]]]
.br
.B loadimage \-s
.I file
[\fIxorigin\fR [\fIyorigin\fR [\fIwidth\fR [\fIheight\fR]]]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B saveimage
writes the screen to
.I file
as a BMP.  With four numbers it writes only that rectangle.
.PP
.B loadimage
draws a BMP onto the screen with its top left corner at
.IR "x y" ,
0 0 by default.
.I ximage
and
.I yimage
take the picture from an offset within the file, so a large BMP can be
used as a sheet to cut from.
.PP
With
.BR \-s ,
.B loadimage
writes a SPRITE to standard output instead of drawing anything, which is
how
.B SPRITE LOADBMP
gets its picture.  The four numbers are a window into the image -
where to start and how much of it to take, all of it from that point by
default - and a window that runs off the edge is refused rather than
clipped.  A sprite needs no display, so
.B \-s
works with the screen in any mode.
.PP
The sprite is written as MMBasic's own conversion of the colours: red's
top bit, green's top two and blue's top bit, with no dithering.  A
sprite is data to be moved about rather than a picture being fitted to
the screen, and a dithered one changes colour when it moves.
.SH THE MODE ARGUMENT
.I mode
is read and then ignored.  It is there so that the arguments after it
stay in the places a program expects; it selects no dithering, and the
colours are matched the same way whatever you pass.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# saveimage shot.bmp
# saveimage part.bmp 160 120 320 240
# loadimage shot.bmp
# loadimage sheet.bmp 0 0 0 64 0
# loadimage -s ship.bmp > ship.spr
# loadimage -s sheet.bmp 64 0 16 16 > tile.spr
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR loadjpg (1),
.BR loadpng (1),
.BR picoctl (1)
